  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiescent weather expected through the end of\nthe week.\n\nVery few if any clouds were evident as of late afternoon thanks\nto sprawling high pressure over the area. South winds around 10\nmph with a few gusts of 15 to 20 mph will become light over land\ntonight (with a bit of a southwest component at slightly higher\nspeeds over the Appalachians). Temperatures tonight will be\nmilder than recent nights given the light south winds.\n\nA dry cold front will approach from the west late tonight, then\ncross the region early Thursday morning. Winds will shift to the\nwest and northwest at 10 to 15 mph with gusts of 20 to 25 mph,\nperhaps occasional/peak gusts near 30 mph near higher terrain.\nDespite the passage of the front, downsloping winds likely\npushing temperatures well into the 70s to near 80 for most.\n\nSeasonable (albeit slightly cooler) temperatures and dry\nconditions will then persist under high pressure Friday through\nFriday night.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Remnants of Tropical Storm Isaias arrive late this \nweekend.\n\nAs of today, the National Hurricane Center is now issuing advisories \non Tropical Storm Isaias down in the Bay of Campeche. This Tropical \ncyclone will need to be monitored closely in the coming days in \nregards to potential impacts to our region late this weekend. There \nremains a great deal of uncertainty, owing to a complex upper-level \npattern ahead of it. A cutoff low over the southern Plains and an \narea of high pressure over Florida will act as the primary steering \nmechanisms in the coming days. The big questions are going to be... \nwhere does it go, and how fast does it get there? A track directly\nover our area is highly uncertain at this time. However, it does\nseem likely to track somewhere up into the MS River Valley and \nthen eventually up towards the Great Lakes/OH Valley as it \ntransitions into a non-tropical low.\n\nAt present, the most likely impacts to our region will be rain. \nHowever, there isn't a great concern for flooding at this time, as \nthe system should be progressive and much of the region could use a \ngood soaking rain to help with drought relief. That being said, this \nsystem is still several days out, so we will be monitoring any \nrainfall trends closely as the time draws closer. There is also a \nnon-zero, but very small, chance that some severe weather could \noccur as a result of the remnants of Isaias. However, again, this \nwill greatly depend on how the upper-level pattern evolves and \ninteracts with Isaias as it moves into our region. The main \nthreat, if it materializes, would be the non-zero threat of a \nquick tornado on Sunday afternoon/evening (mainly near S MD). \n\nAll of this to say, we just simply don't know if Isaias will bring \nany significant impacts our way. However, as is the case with any \ntropical cyclone, it will be important to stay tuned to the forecast \nover the next few days. Monitor hurricanes.gov for details on \nIsaias.",
